<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Qedarites</h1><p class="st-meta">850 BCE – 300 BCE (approx.) · capital Dumat al-Jandal</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The most powerful Arab tribal confederation of the first millennium BCE, controlling desert routes from Syria to the oases of north Arabia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Qedarites (biblical Kedar) appear in Assyrian records from the 8th century BCE as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a>-herding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> of the Syrian desert and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. Their centre was the oasis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dumat-al-jandal">Adummatu</a>, modern Dumat al-Jandal, which held the shrine of their gods. Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabibe">Zabibe</a> paid tribute to Tiglath-Pileser III in 738 BCE, followed by Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsi">Samsi</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They were led by kings and, unusually, by queens who also served as priestesses, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teelhunu">Te'elhunu</a>. The confederation controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> carrying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> from south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> and supplied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> and guides to imperial armies. Repeated revolts brought punitive campaigns: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a> seized their idols, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yauta">Yauta</a> son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazael-of-qedar">Hazael</a> in the 7th century BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> the Qedarites kept their autonomy; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geshem-the-arab">Geshem the Arab</a>, opponent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nehemiah">Nehemiah</a>, and his son Qaynu, named king of Qedar on a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> bowl from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, date to the 5th century BCE. By the 3rd century BCE their place was taken by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nabataean-kingdom">Nabataean Kingdom</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/qedarites.jpg" alt="Qedarites" width="320" height="271"><figcaption>Image: MoeyElan · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qedarite_Map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>850 BCE – 300 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dumat al-Jandal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Zabibe, Samsi, Hazael</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Arabian polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient North Arabian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>, <a href="/countries/jordan">Jordan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kedar, Qedar, Kingdom of Qedar</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-575&amp;focus=qedarites" aria-label="Qedarites on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5877 1308 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Qedarites lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6092 1134h0M6140 1251h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6197 1564h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6197 1564h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Saudi Arabia. <a href="/atlas?year=-575&amp;focus=qedarites">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

